--TEST--
Every name `Core\IO::list` hands back is one `Core\IO::within` admits against the directory it was listed from, and each resolves under that directory's own `Core\IO::canonicalize` — the three members agreeing over a whole listing rather than each answering plausibly on its own line — spec § 14 *Manipulation* and *Resolution*, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An agreement case: one question asked of every member that shares a rule. On
// its own, `list` looks right for any listing at all, and `within` looks right
// for the one path a case hands it. What neither can show alone is that the
// names one produces are names the other accepts -- which is the property a
// program relies on when it lists a directory and then opens what it found.
string $root = Core\IO::canonicalize("tree");
array<string> $entries = Core\IO::list("tree");
int $total = Core\Arr::count($entries) as int;

int $admitted = 0;
int $under = 0;
int $sameAsJoining = 0;

foreach ($entries as string $name) {
    // `within` resolves and then proves. A listing that had handed back a path
    // rather than a name -- `tree/one.txt` -- would still be admitted here,
    // which is why the third count below is the one that tells them apart.
    try {
        string $proved = Core\IO::within("tree", $name);
        $admitted = $admitted + 1;
        if (Core\Str::startsWith($proved, $root)) {
            $under = $under + 1;
        }
        if ($proved == Core\IO::canonicalize(Core\Path::join($root, $name))) {
            $sameAsJoining = $sameAsJoining + 1;
        }
    } catch (RuntimeError $left) {
        echo "refused: ", $name, "\n";
    }
}

echo "entries: ", $total as string, "\n";
echo "admitted by within: ", $admitted as string, "\n";
echo "resolving under the directory: ", $under as string, "\n";
echo "agreeing with the plain join: ", $sameAsJoining as string, "\n";

// The other side of the same bound: a name the directory does not hold is not a
// name `within` invents an answer for, and a name that leaves the directory is
// refused however it is spelled. Without this half every count above is
// satisfied by a `within` that admits everything.
array<string> $notEntries = [
    "../outside.txt",
    "nested/../../outside.txt",
];
int $rejected = 0;
foreach ($notEntries as string $escape) {
    try {
        string $reached = Core\IO::within("tree", $escape);
    } catch (RuntimeError $left) {
        $rejected = $rejected + 1;
    }
}
echo "escapes refused: ", $rejected as string, " of ", Core\Arr::count($notEntries) as string, "\n";
--FILE tree/one.txt--
one
--FILE tree/two.txt--
two
--FILE tree/nested/three.txt--
three
--FILE outside.txt--
outside
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
entries: 3
admitted by within: 3
resolving under the directory: 3
agreeing with the plain join: 3
escapes refused: 2 of 2
